    <div class="msg-payload">
      
          <pre class="wordwrap">Junjie, all, We implemented the manifest and lookup parts on the AIMarket hubs we run, as another way into catalogues that otherwise speak our own protocol: /.well-known/ard.json, the &quot;ard&quot; link relation, POST /search, POST /explore and GET /agents, with publisher trustManifests under did:web. For example: <a href="https://modelmarket.dev/.well-known/ard.json" rel="nofollow">https://modelmarket.dev/.well-known/ard.json</a>. <a href="https://modelmarket.dev/.well-known/ard.json" rel="nofollow">https://modelmarket.dev/.well-known/ard.json</a>. How our catalogue maps onto ARD entries: <a href="https://github.com/alexar76/aimarket-hub/blob/main/docs/ard.md" rel="nofollow">https://github.com/alexar76/aimarket-hub/blob/main/docs/ard.md</a>. <a href="https://github.com/alexar76/aimarket-hub/blob/main/docs/ard.md" rel="nofollow">https://github.com/alexar76/aimarket-hub/blob/main/docs/ard.md</a>. Some notes from doing that: 1. The CDDL in section 5 of draft-bu-dawn-ard-discovery-00 does not compile with the cddl tool: it redefines &quot;any&quot; and &quot;null&quot;, which the RFC 8610 prelude already defines. With those lines removed, and the url/data group choice written as two map alternatives (the tool stops on it inside the entry map), our manifest, a list page, a search request and a search response validate. Because of the open &quot;* tstr =&gt; any&quot; member, the CDDL cannot express that an entry carries exactly one of url and data. 2. Prose and CDDL differ on what is required: the trustManifest &quot;subject&quot; and &quot;signature&quot; are MUST in section 3.5 and optional in the CDDL, and &quot;query.text&quot; is MUST for /search (4.2.2.1) but optional in &quot;query-model&quot;. 3. The DNS owner names differ between the documents: initial-contact 4.2.1 uses <a href="http://_index._agents.example.com" rel="nofollow">_index._agents.example.com</a>, while discovery 4.1 refers to _entries._agents and _search._agents. 4. &quot;Detached JWS&quot; could say whether the payload enters the signing input base64url-encoded (RFC 7515, Appendix F) or unencoded (RFC 7797, b64=false). A test vector would settle it. We used Appendix F. 5. ES256 is mandatory to implement. A publisher whose key is Ed25519 signs with EdDSA, and an ES256-only verifier will reject it. Making EdDSA (RFC 8037) mandatory to implement as well would spare such publishers a second key. 6. (identifier, version) is the deduplication key, but &quot;version&quot; is optional and rarely moves in practice: in our records of public MCP servers, 81.3% of tool-definition changes kept the same serverInfo.version <a href="https://histor.modelmarket.dev/datasets/mcp-protocol-eras-2026-11-01/" rel="nofollow">https://histor.modelmarket.dev/datasets/mcp-protocol-eras-2026-11-01/</a>) <a href="https://histor.modelmarket.dev/datasets/mcp-protocol-eras-2026-11-01/" rel="nofollow">https://histor.modelmarket.dev/datasets/mcp-protocol-eras-2026-11-01/</a>) The digest is the reliable change signal and might deserve a SHOULD. 7. GET /agents: the response body and the filter syntax in the query string are not specified. We return {&quot;entries&quot;: [...], &quot;nextPageToken&quot;: ...} and read a repeated parameter as OR. 8. search-result-item has no version member, while a signed result&#x27;s trustManifest subject includes version and section 3.5.1 has verifiers match the subject against the entry. A registry that returns only the listed members leaves its signed results unverifiable; we ran into this in our first deployment. Adding &quot;? version&quot; to search-result-item would close it. Aleksandr Artamokhov
03.11.2026, 22:23, Junjie Bu &lt; mailto:jbu=40google.com@dmarc.ietf.org <a href="mailto:jbu%3D40google.com@dmarc.ietf.org">jbu=40google.com@dmarc.ietf.org</a> &gt;
Hi, We have submitted a set of drafts documenting an open framework, Agentic Resource Discovery (ARD), for publishing and discovering agent capabilities (such as MCP servers, additional agents, skills, plugins, and APIs) across diverse networks:
</pre>

      
